<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<cfsSimulation xmlns="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation"
xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation
https://opencfs.gitlab.io/cfs/xml/CFS-Simulation/CFS.xsd">

  <documentation>
    <title>Feature mapping via Python with exact Hessian</title>
    <authors>
      <author>Fabian Wein</author>
    </authors>
    <date>2026-07-31</date>
    <references>Wein; tracking paper 2026</references>
    <isVerified>no</isVerified>
    <description>
      We have exact Hessian for compliance and feature mapping in C++ (hessian_compliance).
      This test does it via the Python interface and verifies the Hessian against finite differences (fd_check="true").
      It is meant to be a template for other functions (e.g. output, ...) to be implemented in Python 
      with exact Hessian first.

      Cantilever compliance minimization with a volume constraint on a feature mapping design,
      driven by the true Newton Hessian: the exact geometric terms from cfs.evalhessian plus the
      exact state-curvature block 2 B^T Z, computed via the shape Jacobian D (cfs.get_shape_jacobian),
      the pseudo loads b_i = dK/ds_i u (cfs.apply_dk_drho) and N_s back-substitutions against the
      already factorized state matrix (cfs.solve_state) - no extra FE systems.

      The objective is the generic share/python kernel observed_objective.py wrapping the C++
      compliance (observation with observeGradient="true"); the shared drivers hessian_scipy.py /
      hessian_ipopt.py assemble H = H_geo + 2 B^T Z automatically in their 'observation' mode.

      The native volume constraint has an exact Hessian via the zero rho-curvature of linear
      functions (Function::IsLinearInDensity).
    </description>
  </documentation>

  <!-- generic kernel: wrap the named cfs function as a python objective -->
  <python file="observed_objective.py" path="cfs:share:python" />

  <fileFormats>
    <input>
      <!-- internal regular mesh generator: unit square, named nodes left/right/... -->
      <regular geometryType="plane" region="mech">
        <box xMax="1.0" yMax="1.0"/> 
        <elements nx="12"/>
      </regular>
    </input>
    <output>
      <hdf5/>
      <info/>
    </output>
    <materialData file="mat.xml" format="xml"/>
  </fileFormats>

  <domain geometryType="plane">
    <regionList>
      <region material="99lines" name="mech"/>
    </regionList>
  </domain>

  <sequenceStep index="1">
    <analysis>
      <static/>
    </analysis>

    <pdeList>
      <mechanic subType="planeStress">
        <regionList>
          <region name="mech"/>
        </regionList>

        <bcsAndLoads>
           <fix name="left">
              <comp dof="x"/>
              <comp dof="y"/>
           </fix>
           <force name="bottom_right">
             <comp dof="y" value="-1"/>
           </force>
        </bcsAndLoads>
        <storeResults>
          <elemResult type="mechPseudoDensity">
            <allRegions/>
          </elemResult>
        </storeResults>
      </mechanic>
    </pdeList>
  </sequenceStep>

  <optimization>
    <costFunction type="python" task="minimize">
      <python name="compliance_py" init="obj_init" eval="obj_eval" grad="obj_grad" curvature="obj_curvature" script="kernel">
        <option key="observation" value="compliance"/>
      </python>
    </costFunction>

    <!-- the wrapped C++ compliance: evaluated and differentiated on demand from the python objective -->
    <constraint type="compliance" mode="observation" observeGradient="true"/>

    <!-- native volume constraint; linear="false" is the optimizer semantics (non-linear in the
         shape variables, enforced by FeaturedDesign), while the exact Hessian comes from the zero
         rho-curvature (Function::IsLinearInDensity) -->
    <constraint type="volume" bound="upperBound" value="0.35" linear="false"/>

    <!-- shared second-order driver: the 'observation' option enables the exact state-curvature
         block (see the documentation above). The ONLY test content is fd_check="true": with the
         exact state block the FULL objective Hessian is asserted against finite differences
         (verified rel. error ~1e-9). The three scipy iterations are just a short deterministic run.

         NOTE scipy trust-constr handles the INDEFINITE exact Hessian poorly on this problem (early
         infeasible excursion, then trust region collapse without reaching a KKT point), so further
         iterations buy nothing - that collapse is why this is capped at three. For real convergence
         use hessian_ipopt.py below: Ipopt's inertia correction is built for exact indefinite
         Hessians; see hessian.tex in this directory for those runs. -->
    <optimizer type="python" maxIterations="3">
      <python file="hessian_scipy.py" path="cfs:share:python">
        <option key="observation" value="compliance"/>
        <option key="fd_check" value="true"/>
        <option key="gtol" value="1e-4"/>
        <option key="xtol" value="1e-9"/>
        <option key="initial_tr_radius" value="0.1"/>
      </python>
      <!-- real use native IPOPT with hessian=exact or python with hessian_ipopt.py based on cyipopt (not on the CI runners):
      <python file="hessian_ipopt.py" path="cfs:share:python">
        <option key="observation" value="compliance"/>
        <option key="tol" value="1e-6"/>
      </python> -->
    </optimizer>

    <!-- boundary is the C^2 quintic; the exact geometric Hessian needs H'' -->
    <ersatzMaterial region="mech" material="mechanic" method="featureMapping">
      <featureMapping transition="0.1" boundary="quintic" order="3" combine="p-norm" p="3">
        <pill>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.05" upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.1"  upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.95" upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.1"  upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <profile initial="0.08" lower="0.05" upper="0.25"/>
        </pill>
        <pill>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.05" upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.9"  upper="1" lower="0" tip="start"/>
          <node dof="x" initial="0.95" upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <node dof="y" initial="0.15" upper="1" lower="0" tip="end"/>
          <profile initial="0.08" lower="0.05" upper="0.25"/>
        </pill>
      </featureMapping>
      <design name="density" initial=".5" physical_lower="1e-3" upper="1.0"/>

      <transferFunction type="identity" application="mech"/>
      <export save="all" write="iteration"/>
    </ersatzMaterial>
    <commit mode="forward" stride="1"/>
  </optimization>
</cfsSimulation>
